---
/**
 * /security: the canonical security page. Audit facts mirror
 * docs.ophis.fi/audits in scope (upstream CoW audits plus scoped internal/tool-assisted
 * reviews of Ophis-specific changes). No external audit firm is named
 * because none is engaged; do not add one here without a verifiable public
 * relationship. MEV claims keep the qualified wording from the homepage FAQ.
 */
import ContentPage from '../layouts/ContentPage.astro'
import AuditMethodology from '../components/AuditMethodology.astro'
import { canonicalPage } from '../data/canonicalPages'
import { SOVEREIGN_CHAIN_LIST as sovereignList } from '../data/chains'

const { updated } = canonicalPage('/security/')

const FAQS = [
  {
    q: 'Is Ophis custodial?',
    a: `Signing keys stay with the trader or agent. Standard signed ERC-20 orders keep funds in the wallet until settlement. Native-token orders, bridge routes and optional external OTC escrow can require deposits and gas, with separate recovery rules. Review each wallet authorization.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is Ophis audited?',
    a: `Upstream CoW audits cover relevant shared code, not every Ophis modification or deployment. Ophis-specific allowlist and settlement-buffer changes have internal/tool-assisted reviews. The linked reports define their scope. Using audit skills and tools is not an organizational audit or endorsement by their authors; no external-firm audit is claimed for the fork-specific pieces.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'Is MEV protection guaranteed?',
    a: `Batch-auction orders settle through CoW Protocol, which are designed to protect trades from MEV such as front-running and sandwiching by settling at a uniform clearing price. An order can never be settled below its signed limit price. Batch-auction settlement mitigates MEV by design; it is not an absolute guarantee against all adversarial conditions.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How should an autonomous agent trade safely?',
    a: `Build a bounded order with a hard limit price, pin the receiver to the owner, and resolve supported domains via @ophis/sdk or MCP list_chains before signing. On ${sovereignList} settlement is non-canonical. SDK v0.4.3 and the hosted MCP server include Arc (5042). Set an expiry and limit the funds and permissions available to a signing key.`,
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I report a vulnerability?',
    a: `Follow the disclosure policy in SECURITY.md in the public repository. Report privately first; do not open a public issue for an exploitable finding.`,
  },
]
---

<ContentPage
  title="Security | Ophis"
  description="How Ophis handles security: non-custodial EIP-712 intents, per-chain settlement deployments and scoped reviews, qualified MEV protection, agent safety rules, and vulnerability disclosure."
  path="/security/"
  heading="Security"
  lede="Wallet-authorized trading with signed execution limits. Understand the differences between offchain orders, deposits and bridge routes, and the scope of security reviews."
  updated={updated}
  faqs={FAQS}
>
  <p>
    <strong>The short version:</strong> standard signed orders enforce your sell amount,
    receiver and minimum buy amount in the settlement contract. This does not remove bridge,
    token-issuer, wallet-compromise or infrastructure risk. Native-token and bridge deposits
    have different expiry and recovery rules. Review the scope of each linked security report.
  </p>

  <AuditMethodology />

  {FAQS.map((f) => (
    <section>
      <h2>{f.q}</h2>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </section>
  ))}

